<p><strong>A5 Wagyu</strong> has become so famous that it can sound like the name of a beef category. In reality, A5 combines two separate parts of Japan's grading system: a letter describing estimated carcass yield and a number describing meat quality.</p>
<p>The Japan Meat Grading Association separates yield from quality, which is essential for understanding grades such as A4, B5 or C5 and for avoiding the idea that the letter A simply means “best tasting.”</p>
<h2>What do A, B and C mean?</h2>
<p>The letters are <strong>yield grades</strong>. A indicates yield above the standard, B indicates standard yield and C indicates yield below the standard. They do not directly score flavour, tenderness or marbling.</p>
<h2>What do quality grades 1 to 5 mean?</h2>
<p>The number represents meat quality, with 5 the highest. Four groups are assessed: marbling; meat colour and brightness; firmness and texture; and fat colour, brightness and quality.</p>
<p>The final quality grade is constrained by the lowest-scoring criterion, so spectacular marbling alone cannot produce quality grade 5 if another criterion falls short.</p>
<h2>How does BMS fit in?</h2>
<p>The Beef Marbling Standard runs from 1 to 12. BMS 8–12 satisfy the marbling criterion for quality grade 5; 5–7 for grade 4; 3–4 for grade 3; 2 for grade 2; and 1 for grade 1.</p>
<p>See our <a href="/en/wagyu-bms-marbling-scale-1-12/">BMS guide</a> for more detail.</p>
<h2>Why can two A5 products be different?</h2>
<p>One A5 carcass may have BMS 8 while another has BMS 12. Both are yield A and quality 5, but marbling intensity differs. Cut anatomy also changes what you see in individual steaks.</p>
<h2>What does A4 mean?</h2>
<p>A4 combines yield A and quality 4. The marbling criterion for grade 4 corresponds to BMS 5–7. Some diners prefer this balance because it can feel less rich than extreme A5.</p>
<h2>What does B5 mean?</h2>
<p>B5 is yield B and quality 5. It demonstrates why letter and number must be read separately: the quality number remains the maximum even though yield is not A.</p>
<h2>Is A5+ an official higher grade?</h2>
<p>No. The official system ends at A5. Some retailers use A5+ commercially to refer to very high BMS or other characteristics, so buyers should ask what the plus sign actually means.</p>
<h2>The grade belongs to the carcass</h2>
<p>A5 is assigned to the carcass, not independently to each retail steak. Ribeye and tenderloin from the same animal still have different anatomy, fat distribution and eating characteristics.</p>
<h2>Meat colour matters</h2>
<p>Colour and brightness are assessed against standards. The goal is not simply darker or lighter red; it is whether the meat falls within the appropriate quality range.</p>
<h2>Firmness and texture matter too</h2>
<p>Muscle structure is another part of the assessment. High marbling cannot simply override a lower result in firmness and texture.</p>
<h2>Fat is also evaluated</h2>
<p>Fat colour, brightness and quality form their own criterion. This shows that Japanese grading is multidimensional rather than a single marbling contest.</p>
<h2>Regional brand and grade are different</h2>
<p>A5 does not mean Kobe, Miyazaki or Matsusaka. Grade describes the carcass; a regional brand applies additional origin and production rules.</p>
<h2>How to read a product page</h2>
<p>Check origin first, then the letter-and-number grade, then BMS if provided. After that, look at cut, weight, format and traceability. A marbling photograph alone cannot supply all of this information.</p>
<h2>The conclusion</h2>
<p><strong>Japanese grading combines yield and quality, and A5 is only one of fifteen possible combinations.</strong> A means above-standard yield; 5 is the highest quality grade based on marbling, meat colour, firmness and texture, and fat characteristics.</p>
<p>Once letter, number and BMS are separated, the system becomes a practical buying tool rather than a mysterious premium label.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Is A5 the highest grade?</h3><p>It combines the highest yield category A with the highest quality grade 5.</p>
<h3>Is B5 worse quality than A5?</h3><p>Both are quality grade 5; their yield grades differ.</p>
<h3>Does BMS 12 guarantee A5?</h3><p>No. Other quality criteria must also qualify and yield must be A.</p>
<h3>Is there an A6?</h3><p>No. The official quality scale ends at 5.</p>
